REGIONAL REGULATION OF BITUNG CITY

NUMBER 8 OF 2019

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE IN BITUNG CITY

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF BITUNG,

Considering:

a. that the guarantee of respect, protection and fulfillment of Human Rights for every Indonesian Citizen in the Region is a duty that must be carried out by the Regional Government;
b. that Bitung City as a rapidly developing industrial city as one of the economic centers in North Sulawesi Province, has created many job opportunities so that many people from various areas outside Bitung City come to work within the Bitung City area and have the potential to create social friction which can result in the vulnerability of women and children to become victims of acts of violence;
c. that in the framework of implementing regional autonomy which refers to Article 12 paragraph (2) letter b and Article 236 of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government, particularly in the field of women's empowerment and child protection which is included in concurrent government affairs that are the authority of Regional Government, namely mandatory government affairs not related to basic services, the Region establishes a Regional Regulation;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children from Acts of Violence in Bitung City;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1990 concerning the Establishment of the Second Level Regional Municipality of Bitung (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1990 Number 52, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3421);
4. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as has been amended several times most recently by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
6. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
10. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 64);
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection of the Republic of Indonesia Number 01 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards (SPM) for Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 56);
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning the Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;
17. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 2036); as has been amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 120 of 2018 concerning the Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 80 of 2015 concerning the Formulation of Regional Legal Products (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 157);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF BITUNG CITY

and

MAYOR OF BITUNG

DECIDES:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE IN BITUNG CITY.

Article 2

The scope of this regional regulation includes:
a. acts of violence;
b. implementation of protection;
c. implementation of prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
d. implementation of services, assistance and recovery for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
e. institutions;
f. reunification;
g. empowerment and rehabilitation;
h. guidance and supervision; and
i. financing.

Article 3

The implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence is carried out based on the following principles:
a. respect for Human Rights possessed by women and children;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. the best interests of women and children.

Article 4

The purpose and objectives of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence include:
a. prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
b. handling services, assistance and recovery for women and children who are victims of acts of violence;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment of women and rehabilitation of children who are victims of acts of violence.

CHAPTER II

ACTS OF VIOLENCE

Article 5

1. The Regional Government is obliged to protect every woman and child in the Region from various kinds of acts of violence.
2. Acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) are in the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

Article 8

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (2) letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and
d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article 9

Neglect as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (2) letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the unfulfillment of a child's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for or take care of a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a wife within the scope of her household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract, he is obliged to provide livelihood, care or maintenance to his wife; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence, by restricting and/or prohibiting a wife from working properly inside or outside the home so that the wife is under the control of her husband.

Article 11

Implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence is carried out by:
a. Regional Government;
b. community;
c. family; and
d. parents.

Article 12

1. The Regional Government implements protection efforts for women and children from acts of violence through:
a. implementing protection policies for women and children from acts of violence applied by the Government;
b. establishing policies, programs and activities for protection of women and children from acts of violence;
c. establishment of PPT;
d. ensuring the implementation of ease of service to women and children who become victims of acts of violence;
e. seeking effectiveness and efficiency for the recovery process of women and children who become victims of acts of violence; and
f. seeking the creation of cooperation and coordination in efforts to recover women and children who become victims of acts of violence.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the Regional Government establishes action programs and activities for protection of women and children from acts of violence in 1 (one) RAD as a basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children who become victims of acts of violence.
3. RAD as referred to in paragraph (2) is part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan.
4. RAD as referred to in paragraph (2) is further regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 13

1. Forms of community participation in protecting women and children from acts of violence, include:
a. forming Family Partners at the kelurahan level;
b. forming Women and Child Protection Units within community organizations;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
d. providing first aid to women and children who become victims of acts of violence; and
e. reporting to the competent authority if violence occurs in their environment against women and children who become victims of acts of violence.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector and mass media.
3. Forms of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), are implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 16

1. Implementation of protection for women from acts of violence is carried out through activities:
a. prevention of acts of violence;
b. assistance and recovery services for women who become victims of acts of violence;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment of women who become victims of acts of violence.
2. Implementation of protection of children from acts of violence is carried out through activities:
a. prevention of acts of violence;
b. handling services for children who become victims of acts of violence;
c. reunification; and
d. rehabilitation of children who become victims of acts of violence.
3. Implementation of protection as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is carried out by the Regional Government through the Agency.

Article 17

Implementation of prevention of acts of violence against women and children is carried out by:
a. Regional Government;
b. community;
c. family; and
d. parents.

Article 18

1. The Regional Government implements efforts to prevent acts of violence against women and children through:
a. implementation of policies for prevention of acts of violence against women and children in accordance with policies established by the Government;
b. establishment of policies, programs and activities for prevention of acts of violence against women and children;
c. establishment of PPT;
d. raising awareness among families, parents and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling;
e. increasing the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
f. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
g. opening employment opportunities for women;
h. seeking participation and concern of the community to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
i. developing and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
j. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care about women and children; and
k. opening complaint posts for protection and prevention of acts of violence against women and children.
2. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the fields of:
a. women's empowerment and child protection;
b. health;
c. education;
d. manpower;
e. social;
f. mental and spiritual; and
g. peace and order.
3. Prevention by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (2) is coordinated by the Agency.
4. Prevention of violence by regional apparatus as referred to in paragraph (2) is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on RAD.

Article 20

Forms of community participation in preventing acts of violence against women and children, include:
a. provision of safe houses and/or shelters;
b. establishment and management of orphanages for children;
c. establishment of child rehabilitation centers;
d. active supervision of violence against women and children and reporting to the competent authorities; and
e. providing assistance to victims.

Article 21

Families have the responsibility to prevent acts of violence against women and children, including:
a. maintaining harmony in family relationships;
b. providing assistance to victims;
c. reporting every form of domestic violence to the competent authorities; and
d. assisting the government in efforts to rehabilitate and reunify victims.

Article 23

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women is carried out through activities:a. formation of networks;
b. implementation of coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community, including the private sector and community organizations; and
c. socialization of laws and regulations governing the protection of women.
2. Prevention of acts of violence against children is carried out through activities:
a. formation of networks;
b. implementation of coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns with the community including the private sector and existing community organizations;
c. socialization of laws and regulations governing the protection of children; and
d. education to parents.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) is carried out by involving:
a. families;
b. the community;
c. the private sector;
d. community organizations; and
e. educational institutions.

Article 25

1. Every woman and child who becomes a victim of acts of violence has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. optimal services;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and community member.
2. The Regional Government guarantees the fulfillment of the rights of women and children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1).

Article 26

Children who are victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 25, receive special rights as follows:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 31

PPT consists of elements:
a. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of health;
b. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of education;
c. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of social affairs;
d. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of trade;
e. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of population;
f. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection;
g. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of community empowerment;
h. regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of public peace and order;
i. City Police Resort; and
j. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Article 34

Forms of PPT services for women and children who are victims of acts of violence, include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of women and children who are victims of acts of violence carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services;
d. legal services to assist women and children who are victims of acts of violence in undergoing legal processes; and
e. economic independence services in the form of skills training and providing economic access so that women and children who are victims of acts of violence can be independent.

Article 37

The provision of services and assistance to women and children who are victims of acts of violence shall be conducted with the principles of:
a. fast, safe and empathetic;
b. guarantee of confidentiality;
c. easily accessible; and
d. free of charge.

Article 41

Rehabilitation of children who are victims of acts of violence as referred to in Article 17 letter d shall be carried out in the form of:
a. legal aid services;
b. health rehabilitation services;
c. social rehabilitation services; and/or
d. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 4

The intent and purpose of providing protection for women and children from acts of violence is to:
a. prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children from acts of violence; and
d. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
